C = [
("h1", "5. Gifts and Offices Are Not One List"),
("p", "Claim: Paul gives three lists of what God has set in the church, and they are not the same list. None is a list of offices. The two offices he names in the local assembly are given elsewhere, in the letters to Timothy and Titus and in the greeting to Philippi."),
("sub", "5.1 Three lists"),
("p", "The first is in 1 Corinthians 12: “And God hath set some in the church, first apostles, secondarily prophets, thirdly teachers, after that miracles, then gifts of healings, helps, governments, diversities of tongues” (1 Corinthians 12:28). The second is in Ephesians 4: “And he gave some, apostles; and some, prophets; and some, evangelists; and some, pastors and teachers” (Ephesians 4:11). The third is in Romans 12: “whether prophecy, let us prophesy according to the proportion of faith; Or ministry, let us wait on our ministering: or he that teacheth, on teaching; Or he that exhorteth, on exhortation: he that giveth, let him do it with simplicity; he that ruleth, with diligence; he that sheweth mercy, with cheerfulness” (Romans 12:6–8)."),
("p", "The lists overlap and differ. Apostles, prophets and teachers are in the first two. Prophecy and teaching are in the first and third. Evangelists and pastors are only in the second. Miracles, healings, helps, governments and tongues are only in the first. Giving, exhorting and showing mercy are only in the third. Paul does not say that any one list is the complete set. He gives each for its own argument: the first to show the Body’s need of its many members, the second to show what the ascended Christ gave for the Body’s growth, and the third to show that each member is to use what he has."),
("sub", "5.2 Gifts are of persons and of works"),
("p", "The lists mix two kinds of thing. “Apostles” and “prophets” are persons. “Miracles” and “healings” are works. “Helps” and “governments” name what is done, not who does it. In Romans 12 the same variation holds: “he that teacheth, on teaching” names a person and a work together. Thomas Constable reads the Ephesians list in this way, and the volume agrees with his statement as a report of how Paul uses the words: “It is probably according to their spiritual gifts, rather than their offices, that Paul was thinking of these individuals.”"),
("p", "The point is a careful one. It does not say that an apostle or a teacher held no standing. It says that Paul’s lists do not use the language of appointment. They use the language of giving and setting: “gave,” “hath set,” “having then gifts differing.” The language of appointment appears in other passages, which the following chapters take up."),
("sub", "5.3 What the lists say about the first two"),
("p", "Apostles and prophets stand first in two lists. Paul says in Ephesians 2:20 that the household of God is “built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Jesus Christ himself being the chief corner stone.” The volume reports the sentence as written. It does not say when the foundation was complete, and it does not say whether there are now persons of either name. It also does not decide whether the lists of 1 Corinthians 12 and Ephesians 4 are of the same persons. The volume’s ruling is to report what the passages say about the working of the gifts, not to settle whether any gift has ceased. Paul says in 1 Corinthians 13:8 that “whether there be prophecies, they shall fail; whether there be tongues, they shall cease; whether there be knowledge, it shall vanish away,” and in 13:10 that this is “when that which is perfect is come.” The volume reports the sentences and gives no date for the coming of that which is perfect."),
("sub", "5.4 The pastor in Paul"),
("p", "The word “pastors” in Paul’s letters is found in one place, Ephesians 4:11. It is given there with “teachers,” in one clause. Thomas Constable notes two ways the clause may be read: “Probably the phrase describes the overseers of local churches who both pastor and teach,” and, as another possibility, “the Greek construction may describe two types of gifted people whose ministries are among settled congregations in contrast to the itinerant ministries of the apostles, prophets, and evangelists.” The volume sets the two readings side by side as he does and notes what the passage itself leaves unsaid: it does not call the pastors overseers or elders. Paul does use a related idea elsewhere. He tells the elders of Ephesus to “feed the church of God” (Acts 20:28), and the verb is the shepherd’s verb. The volume reports the connection and does not build an office out of it."),
("ctbl", "The three lists compared.", [
    ("Gift or person", "1 Cor 12:28", "Eph 4:11", "Rom 12:6–8"),
    ("Apostles", "first", "named", "—"),
    ("Prophets", "secondarily", "named", "prophecy"),
    ("Teachers", "thirdly", "with pastors", "he that teacheth"),
    ("Evangelists; pastors", "—", "named", "—"),
    ("Miracles; healings; tongues", "named", "—", "—"),
    ("Helps; governments", "named", "—", "he that ruleth"),
    ("Ministry; exhorting; giving; mercy", "—", "—", "named"),
  ], [0.34, 0.22, 0.22, 0.22]),
("sub", "5.5 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "Paul gives three lists, they differ, and none is stated to be complete.",
    "The lists use the language of giving and setting, not the language of appointment.",
    "The volume gives no date for the end of any gift and decides nothing about cessation.",
    "“Pastors” is a word of one verse; the volume does not build an office from it.",
  ]),
]
